3 Critical Individual Reflection
Working through this financial technology platform highlights the clear operational trade-offs required in database engineering. While software engineering often values high write availability and eventual consistency, banking workloads demand strict consistency. Implementing high-throughput P2P transfers shows that fast performance cannot come at the expense of correct, atomic operations.
Moving from single-instance relational designs to a sharded, distributed polyglot system introduces real challenges around network delays, split-brain risks, and cross-shard locking. The most important takeaway from this architecture is that no single database engine fits all operational needs. Balancing relational systems for money movement with document stores for flexible audit logs and graph databases for fraud detection delivers the best combination of security, compliance, and real-time performance.

